-- Listening Lesson 14 step by step (IELTS-14 v0.3.0), steps 1-3 of 6: five guided steps and one mixed check, in the Lesson 6 format
-- (LEARN FIRST worked example, an explanation for every answer, the transcript only after submitting). Audio is cut
-- from the owner-approved lesson recording (no new speech) and stored in the private ielts-targeted-listening bucket
-- by exact SHA-256. Owner instruction 2026-10-28 15:17 (Asia/Bishkek): 'Go next'. Named-student pilot with
-- requires_review; nothing is released. Source: docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_LISTENING_06.json.
do $steps$
declare tA constant text := 'PROFESSOR GRANT: Come in, Amira, Josh. So, how''s the presentation coming along?
JOSH: Getting there, I think. The survey was harder than we expected, though.
PROFESSOR GRANT: In what way?
AMIRA: Well, we were worried at first that we wouldn''t get enough responses, but in the end thirty-two businesses replied, which was more than we needed.
JOSH: The real problem was the wording. Several owners thought ''engagement'' meant how many followers they had, so their answers to question four don''t really tell us anything.
AMIRA: And we sent it out too late. Most replies came in last week, so we''ve had very little time to analyse them.
PROFESSOR GRANT: Those are both useful things to mention when you discuss the limitations. Now, let''s go through the structure. You''ve got a background section first?
AMIRA: Yes, but it''s over four minutes long at the moment.
PROFESSOR GRANT: That''s far too much for a fifteen-minute talk. I''d cut it to about one minute. The audience will know the basics.
JOSH: OK, we''ll shorten it. Then we have the survey results.
PROFESSOR GRANT: What''s the most surprising finding?
AMIRA: That the smallest businesses, the ones with only one or two staff, post more often than the larger ones.
PROFESSOR GRANT: That''s a strong hook. Why not open with it, rather than burying it in the middle?
JOSH: Good idea. So the results go at the start.
AMIRA: What about the slide on the history of social media? I spent quite a long time on that.
PROFESSOR GRANT: I can see it was a lot of work, but it doesn''t connect to your question. I''d leave it out altogether.
AMIRA: Fair enough.
JOSH: And the recommendations at the end? We were going to leave them as they are.
PROFESSOR GRANT: They''re sensible, but rather general. Each one would be more convincing with a real example from one of the businesses you surveyed.
JOSH: Right, we can do that.
AMIRA: One more thing about the results. We''ve used pie charts for everything at the moment.'; tB constant text := 'PROFESSOR GRANT: Pie charts work well for showing what share of your sample each size of business made up. But for how often each group posts, you''re asking the audience to compare amounts across groups, and that''s much easier to see as bars side by side.
JOSH: So should we just turn everything into bar charts?
PROFESSOR GRANT: No, the pie chart is the right choice for business size.
AMIRA: Or we could leave the pie charts as they are and add the exact figures to each slice.
PROFESSOR GRANT: Figures help, but the audience would still be comparing slices from different pies. It''s only the posting chart that needs to change.
JOSH: So we keep the pie chart for business size and change the posting one to a bar chart.
PROFESSOR GRANT: Exactly. And how long will you spend on the limitations you mentioned?
JOSH: Honestly, I thought we''d skip them. We''re short of time as it is.
AMIRA: But they''re the reason question four doesn''t tell us much. If we don''t say so, someone will ask. Could they go at the very end, after the recommendations?
PROFESSOR GRANT: I''d put them just before the recommendations, so that each recommendation can show how you''d deal with a limitation. It doesn''t need long: thirty seconds would show you''ve thought critically about your own data.
JOSH: Fine, a short slide on limitations, then, just before the recommendations. By the way, I heard the deadline had been moved to next Friday.
PROFESSOR GRANT: That''s for the marketing group, I''m afraid. Yours is still this Thursday, as planned.
JOSH: Ah. OK, Thursday it is.
PROFESSOR GRANT: And who''s presenting which part?
JOSH: I was going to do the results and the recommendations, and Amira the background.
AMIRA: Actually, since the background is now so short, wouldn''t it make more sense for me to do the recommendations as well? I interviewed most of the owners, so I know the examples.
JOSH: That makes sense. You take the recommendations, then.
PROFESSOR GRANT: Good. Send me your slides by Wednesday morning if you''d like comments.';
begin
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,audio_path,audio_sha256,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-listening-l21-v1','0.1.0','Listening Lesson 14 step 1 of 6: Who said it?','listening','guided_practice','targeted-listening-06/43c73cde98d80f72fd170bea406d070152a8ebecedb73ec6c0730c363758adc4/l21/BH-TL06-L14A_v1.mp3','43c73cde98d80f72fd170bea406d070152a8ebecedb73ec6c0730c363758adc4','You will hear two students, Amira and Josh, talking to their tutor, Professor Grant, about a presentation. Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then listen to the first half of the discussion. Who says each thing? Choose A, B or C. You may pause or replay.','Learn the three voices in the first few lines, then follow the names: the tutor asks and advises; the students report and react.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "says the survey was harder than they expected", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "19e4299c-70f4-4d30-8726-4416be272056", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Amira"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Josh"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Professor Grant"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "says that thirty-two businesses replied", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "19e4299c-70f4-4d30-8726-4416be272056", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Amira"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Josh"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Professor Grant"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "explains what several owners thought ''engagement'' meant", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "19e4299c-70f4-4d30-8726-4416be272056", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Amira"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Josh"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Professor Grant"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "says the background section is over four minutes long", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "19e4299c-70f4-4d30-8726-4416be272056", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Amira"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Josh"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Professor Grant"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "suggests opening the talk with the most surprising finding", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "4bdeaffb-7390-4b00-9955-69b9da537007", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Amira"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Josh"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Professor Grant"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "spent a long time on the slide about the history of social media", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "19e4299c-70f4-4d30-8726-4416be272056", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Amira"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Josh"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Professor Grant"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS listening exercise; not a full paper or an official IELTS recording.", "suggested_minutes": 7, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-listening-targeted-focus-v1", "passage": "LEARN FIRST: know the voices first\n\nPart 3 has two to four speakers. Many questions depend on WHO says something.\n1. In the first lines, match each voice to a name. Here the tutor names both students: ''Come in, Amira, Josh.''\n2. Note one feature of each voice (for example: the tutor is calm and asks questions).\n3. Follow the roles: the tutor asks and gives advice; the students explain their work and react.\n\nThe speakers:\nA  Amira, a student (Australian accent)\nB  Josh, a student (North American accent)\nC  Professor Grant, the tutor (British accent)\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nYou hear: ''So, how''s the presentation coming along?''\nThe tutor is checking on the students'' work. -> C, Professor Grant\n\nNow listen and answer six questions.", "focus": "Track spoken reference: identify which of three speakers says each thing in a Part 3 discussion."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('teacher_notes', '1 B, Josh: "Getting there, I think. The survey was harder than we expected, though."
2 A, Amira: "in the end thirty-two businesses replied, which was more than we needed".
3 B, Josh: "Several owners thought ''engagement'' meant how many followers they had".
4 A, Amira: "Yes, but it''s over four minutes long at the moment."
5 C, Professor Grant: "That''s a strong hook. Why not open with it, rather than burying it in the middle?"
6 A, Amira: "What about the slide on the history of social media? I spent quite a long time on that."
Read each explanation, then listen to that part again and hear the proof yourself.

